  "chunk": "that models can assess their own outputs but also highlight that there is a\nconsiderable gap between the capabilities of the models and human profiency.\nThis connects to the broader challenge of common sense in AI, which Oren\nEtzioni has called “the dark matter of artificial intelligence” [16]—a quality\nwhose effects are visible everywhere but whose nature remains elusive. Common\nsense encompasses not just vast knowledge about the world, but the ability to\nuse that knowledge flexibly to recognize situations, make predictions, and guide\nactions [8, 18].\nIn this paper, we contribute to the debate by evaluating how state-of-the-art\nLLMs perform onZork, a game that requires players to build coherent mental\nrepresentations, learn from feedback, and adaptively solve a series of puzzles.\nInstead of deploying an RL approach that trains agents through millions of\nepisodes on a specific game, we examine LLMs in a zero-shot setting—mirroring",
